<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viceroy of Kush</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Viceroy of Kush, also called the King's Son of Kush, was the official who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. Accounts of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom describe the office as a semi-autonomous imperial sub-administration. It oversaw the region's gold production, tribute and garrisons.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Provincial governors administered the nomes, while the <b>Viceroy of Kush</b>, the King's Son of Kush, exercised authority over Nubia as a semi-autonomous imperial sub-administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Nubia was governed by a <b>Viceroy of Kush</b>, the “King’s Son of Kush”, Egyptian towns were founded on Sai Island and at Sedeinga, Soleb, Mirgissa and Sesibi, and major ceremonies were held at Jebel Barkal near Napata.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Beneath them served the treasurers, the overseers of granaries and of works, the mayors of the towns, the generals and the <b>Viceroy of Kush</b>, who governed conquered Nubia.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
